WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.192 --> 00:00:18.115
was in Grandpa's study, looking for a book she needed for her history report.

2
00:00:18.135 --> 00:00:23.498
"Aha!" she said, grabbing a book called Alexander the Great.

3
00:00:23.999 --> 00:00:27.081
Just then a leather-bound journal caught her eye.

4
00:00:28.102 --> 00:00:31.424
When she pulled it from the shelf, a photograph fluttered to the floor.

5
00:00:32.316 --> 00:00:36.939
It was a photo of Grandpa standing among mysterious stone doorways.

6
00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:39.120
Grandpa looked very young.

7
00:00:40.741 --> 00:00:45.564
The title page of the journal read: Persepolis, 1979.

8
00:00:46.324 --> 00:00:48.366
It was written in Grandpa's handwriting.

9
00:00:48.886 --> 00:00:51.327
Laurie turned the page and started reading.

10
00:00:51.347 --> 00:00:54.770
"'Jim and I just arrived in Iran this morning.

11
00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:58.612
The drive from the airport was uneventful but hot.

12
00:00:59.432 --> 00:01:05.574
I spent the whole bus ride wondering what the scenery might have looked like back in 500 BC—'"

13
00:01:05.594 --> 00:01:09.595
"Laurie?" Grandpa suddenly appeared in the doorway.

14
00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:11.956
"You scared me!"

15
00:01:12.516 --> 00:01:14.716
Laurie cried, slamming the journal shut.

16
00:01:14.736 --> 00:01:16.477
"What are you reading?"

17
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:17.457
he asked.

18
00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:20.598
"Ah, my journal from Iran!

19
00:01:21.098 --> 00:01:27.240
Your great-uncle Jim and I went to Persepolis the year it was declared a UNESCO World Heritage site.

20
00:01:27.968 --> 00:01:30.929
We had a terrific time exploring the ruins there."

21
00:01:30.949 --> 00:01:33.109
"What is Persepolis?"

22
00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:34.430
asked Laurie.

23
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:41.952
"It was the capital of the Persian Empire, which was the largest empire in the world from 559

24
00:01:42.052 --> 00:01:44.393
BC to 330 BC.

25
00:01:45.593 --> 00:01:51.674
It covered most of the Middle East, from Egypt to India, and was made up of many different

26
00:01:51.755 --> 00:02:01.010
provinces. Although Persian rulers tried to do something impossible— unite the known world under one power— they were

27
00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:05.932
nearly successful because they allowed the conquered people to keep their freedoms.

28
00:02:06.972 --> 00:02:15.836
In fact, some call the Cyrus Cylinder—an ancient Persian document written on a clay cylinder—the first declaration of

29
00:02:15.936 --> 00:02:16.657
human rights.

30
00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:23.560
It describes how Cyrus let the people in his empire have freedom of culture and religion.

31
00:02:24.236 --> 00:02:30.279
The ruler who founded the empire and conquered much of its territory was Cyrus the Great.

32
00:02:31.219 --> 00:02:37.442
Many say he began the age of world-empire building, which would continue with the Greeks and Romans.

33
00:02:38.923 --> 00:02:42.624
He had some good ideas about how to create and control an empire.

34
00:02:43.245 --> 00:02:49.067
For example, he knew it was important to make sure the people you conquered were happy so they

35
00:02:49.087 --> 00:02:50.568
would not rise up against you.

36
00:02:51.464 --> 00:02:59.028
So when Cyrus marched his troops into Babylon in 539 BC, he did not burn buildings, steal from

37
00:02:59.088 --> 00:03:01.830
people's homes, or run around killing people.

38
00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.592
He became known as a peaceful and generous ruler.

39
00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:13.156
He also freed the Jews who were held captive in Babylon and allowed them to return to Jerusalem.

40
00:03:14.084 --> 00:03:21.311
Cyrus declared each conquered area a province and asked that all provinces pay him a yearly sum of

41
00:03:21.371 --> 00:03:22.211
gold and silver.

42
00:03:23.012 --> 00:03:27.156
In return he promised to protect them from foreign invaders.

43
00:03:28.137 --> 00:03:33.602
This system worked out nicely for Cyrus, and he managed to build a wealthy empire."

44
00:03:33.622 --> 00:03:36.544
"Did Cyrus build Persepolis?"

45
00:03:37.065 --> 00:03:39.207
Laurie asked, looking back at the photo.

46
00:03:41.024 --> 00:03:48.726
"No, Persepolis was built by a king named Darius the Great, who ruled from 522 BC to 486

47
00:03:48.786 --> 00:03:57.589
BC. Darius was called the Great because he continued to expand the empire's borders, developed an organized system

48
00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:01.810
of rule, and improved the kingdom with major building projects.

49
00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:08.952
In order to stay informed about his empire, Darius needed people to travel and report back to him.

50
00:04:10.128 --> 00:04:15.253
So he built a great road, called the royal road, which connected the provinces.

51
00:04:16.234 --> 00:04:20.057
And this was way before the Romans built their famous roads, mind you.

52
00:04:20.878 --> 00:04:25.602
The royal road was useful for both transportation and communication.

53
00:04:26.563 --> 00:04:32.188
He also finished organizing the provinces— something that was begun during Cyrus' time.

54
00:04:33.009 --> 00:04:36.072
And he appointed governors to manage each province.

55
00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.203
This way he could make sure each province paid him the right amount of gold and silver.

56
00:04:42.843 --> 00:04:48.766
But most famously Darius built a huge palace complex in Persepolis.

57
00:04:49.606 --> 00:04:53.768
This served as the center of government and a guest reception area.

58
00:04:54.589 --> 00:05:02.124
The plans for Persepolis—a city that aimed to impress all visitors with its riches and splendor— were so

59
00:05:02.224 --> 00:05:07.067
grand that Persepolis couldn't be completed within Darius' lifetime.

60
00:05:08.288 --> 00:05:11.069
It was actually finished by his son, the next king.

61
00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:15.672
There were palaces, great halls, a treasury, and more."

62
00:05:15.692 --> 00:05:23.516
"But Grandpa, you wrote: ‘It is a shame that Alexander the Great laid waste to this magnificent city.

63
00:05:24.196 --> 00:05:29.379
I can only imagine what Persepolis might look like today, if only it had been preserved as a

64
00:05:29.439 --> 00:05:33.709
historic treasure,'" Laurie interrupted, reading from the journal.

65
00:05:33.729 --> 00:05:37.872
"I'm writing a report on Alexander the Great for homework.

66
00:05:38.312 --> 00:05:39.853
What did he do to Persepolis?"

67
00:05:40.893 --> 00:05:44.696
"Well, the Persians and the Greeks eventually fought against each other.

68
00:05:45.756 --> 00:05:55.662
Clashes began during Darius' time, continued through his son's rule, and then finally, around 330 BC, the warrior

69
00:05:55.842 --> 00:05:59.344
Alexander the Great invaded and conquered Persia.

70
00:06:00.308 --> 00:06:09.137
When he reached Persepolis, he robbed the treasury, set fire to the palaces, and destroyed the sculptures— especially

71
00:06:09.338 --> 00:06:11.620
any that showed images of Persian kings.

72
00:06:12.561 --> 00:06:18.087
His conquests marked the end of the Persian Empire, and the dawn of the Greek.

73
00:06:19.168 --> 00:06:22.552
Laurie, have you heard of the Greek historian Herodotus?

74
00:06:24.944 --> 00:06:32.470
We know all this history because Herodotus traveled the Middle East collecting stories and recording information.

75
00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:38.834
He had a curious mind and a great interest in the actions and politics of humans.

76
00:06:39.795 --> 00:06:45.759
He wrote about the Persian Empire and Persian wars in a big book called The Histories.

77
00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:53.557
A great writer, he wrote as if he were telling someone a story face-to-face and often mixed in

78
00:06:53.657 --> 00:06:55.699
entertaining stories from his travels.

79
00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:00.783
I wish I could write such interesting histories," Grandpa said.

80
00:07:00.803 --> 00:07:05.467
"But your history stories are just like that, Grandpa!"

81
00:07:05.807 --> 00:07:06.488
Laurie said.

82
00:07:06.508 --> 00:07:08.629
"They're always interesting!"

83
00:07:08.649 --> 00:07:10.571
"Thanks, Laurie."

84
00:07:11.011 --> 00:07:12.052
Grandpa smiled.

85
00:07:12.072 --> 00:07:16.015
"Maybe someday you can help me organize these journal entries.

86
00:07:16.996 --> 00:07:21.680
There's a lot of history in here," he said, lightly tapping his journal.

87
00:07:22.777 --> 00:07:23.899
"I'd be honored."

88
00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:25.022
Laurie grinned.

89
00:07:25.042 --> 00:07:28.591
"But for now I think I'm pretty busy with Alexander."

90
00:07:29.633 --> 00:07:32.059
She picked up the book and walked out of the study.
